/* Verse Games: THE WHOLE COUNSEL · 2 Kings chapters 16-20
   Verse text is filled/verified from the KJV by tools/fill-verses.js */
VG.registerBibleChapters("2kings", [
{n:16, name:"Ahaz and the Assyrian Alliance", seal:{ref:"2 Kings 16:3",verse:"But he walked in the way of the kings of Israel, yea, and made his son to pass through the fire, according to the abominations of the heathen, whom the LORD cast out from before the children of Israel."}, facts:["Did you know Ahaz stripped the silver and gold out of the house of the LORD and his own treasury to bribe Assyria, trading the temple's treasure for a foreign king's protection (2 Kings 16:7-8)?","Did you know Ahaz saw a pagan altar in Damascus and had the high priest Urijah copy it, pushing God's own bronze altar aside to make room for the copy (2 Kings 16:10-14)?","Did you know Ahaz cut apart the bronze bases, the laver, and the great sea of Solomon's temple, dismantling the house of God to impress the king of Assyria (2 Kings 16:17-18)?"], questions:[
{q:"In what year of Pekah's reign did Ahaz begin to reign over Judah?", options:["In the seventeenth year of Pekah the son of Remaliah","In the twelfth year of Pekah the son of Remaliah","In the first year of Pekah the son of Remaliah","In the twentieth year of Pekah the son of Remaliah"], a:0, ref:"2 Kings 16:1",verse:"In the seventeenth year of Pekah the son of Remaliah Ahaz the son of Jotham king of Judah began to reign.", insight:"Scripture dates Judah's kings by Israel's kings; even in division, God kept one story moving."},
{q:"How old was Ahaz when he began to reign?", options:["Twenty years old","Twenty-five years old","Thirty years old","Forty-one years old"], a:0, ref:"2 Kings 16:2",verse:"Twenty years old was Ahaz when he began to reign, and reigned sixteen years in Jerusalem, and did not that which was right in the sight of the LORD his God, like David his father.", insight:"Twenty years old and on the throne; youth does not excuse a man from choosing whom he will serve."},
{q:"How many years did Ahaz reign in Jerusalem?", options:["Sixteen years","Twenty years","Twenty-nine years","Forty-one years"], a:0, ref:"2 Kings 16:2",verse:"Twenty years old was Ahaz when he began to reign, and reigned sixteen years in Jerusalem, and did not that which was right in the sight of the LORD his God, like David his father.", insight:"Sixteen years of influence, and every one of them pulled Judah further from God; time is an amplifier."},
{q:"What did Ahaz do to his own son?", options:["He made his son to pass through the fire, after the abominations of the heathen","He dedicated his son to the temple priests","He sent his son as a hostage to Assyria","He crowned his son king over Edom"], a:0, ref:"2 Kings 16:3",verse:"But he walked in the way of the kings of Israel, yea, and made his son to pass through the fire, according to the abominations of the heathen, whom the LORD cast out from before the children of Israel.", insight:"The worst thing a father can do with his child is offer him to a lie; Ahaz reached that depth."},
{q:"Where did Ahaz sacrifice and burn incense?", options:["In the high places, and on the hills, and under every green tree","Only in the temple courts","In the caves of the wilderness","On the roof of his palace"], a:0, ref:"2 Kings 16:4",verse:"And he sacrificed and burnt incense in the high places, and on the hills, and under every green tree.", insight:"Under every green tree; when worship loses its one appointed place, it spreads into idolatry everywhere."},
{q:"Which two kings besieged Ahaz in Jerusalem?", options:["Rezin king of Syria and Pekah son of Remaliah king of Israel","Tiglath-pileser and Shalmaneser","Hoshea and Elah","Ben-hadad and Hazael"], a:0, ref:"2 Kings 16:5",verse:"Then Rezin king of Syria and Pekah son of Remaliah king of Israel came up to Jerusalem to war: and they besieged Ahaz, but could not overcome him.", insight:"Enemies conspired against Ahaz, but the siege he should have feared was the one sin laid around his own heart."},
{q:"Could the besiegers overcome Ahaz?", options:["No, they besieged him but could not overcome him","Yes, they took the city and carried him captive","They breached the walls but withdrew at night","They starved him into surrender in one year"], a:0, ref:"2 Kings 16:5",verse:"Then Rezin king of Syria and Pekah son of Remaliah king of Israel came up to Jerusalem to war: and they besieged Ahaz, but could not overcome him.", insight:"God still guarded the city Ahaz was busy betraying; grace sometimes shields those who do not deserve it."},
{q:"What city did Rezin recover to Syria during this war?", options:["Elath","Damascus","Gaza","Samaria"], a:0, ref:"2 Kings 16:6",verse:"At that time Rezin king of Syria recovered Elath to Syria, and drave the Jews from Elath: and the Syrians came to Elath, and dwelt there unto this day.", insight:"While Judah's king chased foreign help, Syria took back Elath; faithlessness leaks territory on every front."},
{q:"To whom did Ahaz send messengers for rescue?", options:["Tiglath-pileser king of Assyria","So king of Egypt","The king of Tyre","The king of Babylon"], a:0, ref:"2 Kings 16:7",verse:"So Ahaz sent messengers to Tiglath-pileser king of Assyria, saying, I am thy servant and thy son: come up, and save me out of the hand of the king of Syria, and out of the hand of the king of Israel, which rise up against me.", insight:"Ahaz hired Assyria instead of crying to heaven; paid rescue always costs more than prayer."},
{q:"What did Ahaz call himself in his message to Assyria?", options:["I am thy servant and thy son","I am thy brother and thy ally","I am a king equal to thee","I am thy priest and prophet"], a:0, ref:"2 Kings 16:7",verse:"So Ahaz sent messengers to Tiglath-pileser king of Assyria, saying, I am thy servant and thy son: come up, and save me out of the hand of the king of Syria, and out of the hand of the king of Israel, which rise up against me.", insight:"'Thy servant and thy son'; Ahaz traded his sonship to God for servitude to Assyria."},
{q:"What did Ahaz send as a present to the king of Assyria?", options:["The silver and gold found in the house of the LORD and in the king's treasures","The golden shields of Solomon's temple","The daughters of his household as servants","The tribute of all the cities of Judah"], a:0, ref:"2 Kings 16:8",verse:"And Ahaz took the silver and gold that was found in the house of the LORD, and in the treasures of the king’s house, and sent it for a present to the king of Assyria.", insight:"He emptied God's house to fill Assyria's hands; when you pay the world for help, the bill comes from holy things."},
{q:"What did the king of Assyria do to Damascus?", options:["Took it, carried its people captive to Kir, and slew Rezin","Besieged it for three years and then withdrew","Made a league with it against Israel","Burned its temples but left its king"], a:0, ref:"2 Kings 16:9",verse:"And the king of Assyria hearkened unto him: for the king of Assyria went up against Damascus, and took it, and carried the people of it captive to Kir, and slew Rezin.", insight:"The hired sword worked, and then it would not be dismissed; weapons you hire eventually aim at you."},
{q:"Where did Ahaz go to meet Tiglath-pileser?", options:["To Damascus","To Nineveh","To Samaria","To Carchemish"], a:0, ref:"2 Kings 16:10",verse:"And king Ahaz went to Damascus to meet Tiglath-pileser king of Assyria, and saw an altar that was at Damascus: and king Ahaz sent to Urijah the priest the fashion of the altar, and the pattern of it, according to all the workmanship thereof.", insight:"Ahaz traveled to meet his master; idolaters always go to their idols, and they never come back unchanged."},
{q:"What did Ahaz send to Urijah the priest from Damascus?", options:["The fashion of the altar that was at Damascus","A plan for the temple's restoration","An order to close the temple","The tribute list for Assyria"], a:0, ref:"2 Kings 16:10",verse:"And king Ahaz went to Damascus to meet Tiglath-pileser king of Assyria, and saw an altar that was at Damascus: and king Ahaz sent to Urijah the priest the fashion of the altar, and the pattern of it, according to all the workmanship thereof.", insight:"He imported a pagan design for God's worship; compromise always arrives with blueprints."},
{q:"What did Urijah the priest do?", options:["Built an altar according to all that king Ahaz had sent from Damascus","Refused the king and kept the old altar","Fled from Jerusalem to Egypt","Burned the letter and warned the elders"], a:0, ref:"2 Kings 16:11",verse:"And Urijah the priest built an altar according to all that king Ahaz had sent from Damascus: so Urijah the priest made it against king Ahaz came from Damascus.", insight:"Urijah built what the king sent; priestly hands should never build what politics designs."},
{q:"What did Ahaz offer upon the new altar?", options:["His burnt offering, his meat offering, his drink offering, and the blood of his peace offerings","Only a morning lamb, as the law prescribed","Offerings to Tiglath-pileser himself","Incense to the stars of heaven"], a:0, ref:"2 Kings 16:13",verse:"and he burnt his burnt offering and his meat offering, and poured his drink offering, and sprinkled the blood of his peace offerings, upon the altar.", insight:"He poured every kind of offering on a foreign altar; zeal without obedience is just noise on the wrong fire."},
{q:"What did Ahaz do with the brasen altar that stood before the LORD?", options:["Brought it from the forefront of the house and set it on the north side of the new altar","Melted it down for the Assyrian tribute","Hid it in the temple storehouses","Sent it to Samaria as a gift"], a:0, ref:"2 Kings 16:14",verse:"And he brought also the brasen altar, which was before the LORD, from the forefront of the house, from between the altar and the house of the LORD, and put it on the north side of the altar.", insight:"God's altar got pushed aside for the import; when novelty leads, devotion gets moved to the north side."},
{q:"What did Ahaz command Urijah concerning the great altar?", options:["Burn the morning burnt offering, the evening meat offering, and the king's sacrifices upon it","Use it only for burnt offerings of the poor","Cover it with a cloth and never use it","Break it and sell the brass"], a:0, ref:"2 Kings 16:15",verse:"And king Ahaz commanded Urijah the priest, saying, Upon the great altar burn the morning burnt offering, and the evening meat offering, and the king’s burnt sacrifice, and his meat offering, with the burnt offering of all the people of the land, and their meat offering, and their drink offerings; and sprinkle upon it all the blood of the burnt offering, and all the blood of the sacrifice: and the brasen altar shall be for me to enquire by.", insight:"He regulated worship around his own inventions; when man orders worship, God is no longer ordering man."},
{q:"What did Ahaz do to the temple's bronze vessels?", options:["Cut off the borders of the bases, removed the laver, and took the sea from off the brasen oxen","Carried them all away to Damascus","Gilded them and set them in his palace","Gave them to the priests for safekeeping"], a:0, ref:"2 Kings 16:17",verse:"And king Ahaz cut off the borders of the bases, and removed the laver from off them; and took down the sea from off the brasen oxen that were under it, and put it upon a pavement of stones.", insight:"He dismantled Solomon's work piece by piece; backsliding is usually disassembly, not explosion."},
{q:"Why did Ahaz turn the sabbath covert and the king's entry from the house of the LORD?", options:["For the king of Assyria","For the priests of Baal","For the king of Syria","For the merchants of Jerusalem"], a:0, ref:"2 Kings 16:18",verse:"And the covert for the sabbath that they had built in the house, and the king’s entry without, turned he from the house of the LORD for the king of Assyria.", insight:"The temple's furniture was rearranged for a foreign king's visit; whatever you arrange your life around is what you worship."}
]},
{n:17, name:"The Fall of Samaria", seal:{ref:"2 Kings 17:18",verse:"Therefore the LORD was very angry with Israel, and removed them out of his sight: there was none left but the tribe of Judah only."}, facts:["Did you know Assyria's king resettled Samaria with peoples from Babylon, Cuthah, Ava, Hamath, and Sepharvaim, and when lions attacked them he sent back a captive priest to teach them the God of the land (2 Kings 17:24-28)?","Did you know the new peoples 'feared the LORD and served their own gods,' mixing true worship with idols, a compromise whose echo reached the days of Jesus (2 Kings 17:33,41)?","Did you know Israel's fall was centuries in the making? They walked in all the sins of Jeroboam from the first day of the divided kingdom and never departed from them (2 Kings 17:21-22)."], questions:[
{q:"Who began to reign over Israel in the twelfth year of Ahaz?", options:["Hoshea the son of Elah","Pekah the son of Remaliah","Shalmaneser the son of Pul","Zimri the son of Zerah"], a:0, ref:"2 Kings 17:1",verse:"In the twelfth year of Ahaz king of Judah began Hoshea the son of Elah to reign in Samaria over Israel nine years.", insight:"Hoshea was Israel's last king; every nation has a final chapter, and his was being written."},
{q:"How many years did Hoshea reign in Samaria?", options:["Nine years","Sixteen years","Twenty years","Forty years"], a:0, ref:"2 Kings 17:1",verse:"In the twelfth year of Ahaz king of Judah began Hoshea the son of Elah to reign in Samaria over Israel nine years.", insight:"Nine years on the throne of a dying kingdom; length of reign cannot save a nation that has chosen its end."},
{q:"How did Hoshea's evil compare with the kings of Israel before him?", options:["Evil, but not as the kings of Israel that were before him","Worse than any king before him","The most righteous king Israel ever had","Equal to David in righteousness"], a:0, ref:"2 Kings 17:2",verse:"And he did that which was evil in the sight of the LORD, but not as the kings of Israel that were before him.", insight:"'Not as' the others; milder sin is still sin, and it still invited the same judgment."},
{q:"Which king of Assyria came up against Hoshea?", options:["Shalmaneser","Sennacherib","Tiglath-pileser","Nebuchadnezzar"], a:0, ref:"2 Kings 17:3",verse:"Against him came up Shalmaneser king of Assyria; and Hoshea became his servant, and gave him presents.", insight:"Shalmaneser came up; judgment often arrives with a name and an army, not just a warning."},
{q:"What did Hoshea become to Assyria, and what did he give?", options:["His servant, and gave him presents","His ally, and gave him land","His priest, and gave him idols","His prisoner, and gave him hostages"], a:0, ref:"2 Kings 17:3",verse:"Against him came up Shalmaneser king of Assyria; and Hoshea became his servant, and gave him presents.", insight:"Tribute bought time, not mercy; payments to oppressors never purchase peace."},
{q:"What conspiracy did the king of Assyria find in Hoshea?", options:["He had sent messengers to So king of Egypt, and brought no present to Assyria","He had built an alliance with Judah and Tyre","He had fortified Samaria against the prophets","He had sacrificed to the LORD at Bethel"], a:0, ref:"2 Kings 17:4",verse:"And the king of Assyria found conspiracy in Hoshea: for he had sent messengers to So king of Egypt, and brought no present to the king of Assyria, as he had done year by year: therefore the king of Assyria shut him up, and bound him in prison.", insight:"He schemed behind his master's back; double dealing with kings is how small kings get crushed."},
{q:"How long did the king of Assyria besiege Samaria?", options:["Three years","Seven months","Forty days","One year"], a:0, ref:"2 Kings 17:5",verse:"Then the king of Assyria came up throughout all the land, and went up to Samaria, and besieged it three years.", insight:"Three years of siege; judgment knocked a long time before it broke the door down."},
{q:"In what year of Hoshea was Samaria taken?", options:["The ninth year","The third year","The twelfth year","The twentieth year"], a:0, ref:"2 Kings 17:6",verse:"In the ninth year of Hoshea the king of Assyria took Samaria, and carried Israel away into Assyria, and placed them in Halah and in Habor by the river of Gozan, and in the cities of the Medes.", insight:"The ninth year closed the kingdom; God's patience has a number, and Samaria reached it."},
{q:"Why was Israel carried away into captivity?", options:["They had sinned against the LORD their God, which brought them up out of Egypt","They had refused to pay tribute to Assyria","They had rebelled against the house of David","They had trusted too much in Egypt"], a:0, ref:"2 Kings 17:7",verse:"For so it was, that the children of Israel had sinned against the LORD their God, which had brought them up out of the land of Egypt, from under the hand of Pharaoh king of Egypt, and had feared other gods,", insight:"The root was sin against the God who rescued them; ingratitude has a memory, and so does justice."},
{q:"What did Israel do secretly against the LORD?", options:["Things that were not right, and built them high places in all their cities","Offered secret prayers in their chambers","Hid the law of Moses from their children","Buried their gold to avoid tribute"], a:0, ref:"2 Kings 17:9",verse:"And the children of Israel did secretly those things that were not right against the LORD their God, and they built them high places in all their cities, from the tower of the watchmen to the fenced city.", insight:"Secret sin became public shrines; what starts hidden in the heart ends built on every hill."},
{q:"What did they set up on every high hill and under every green tree?", options:["Images and groves","Altars to the LORD","Memorials to the patriarchs","Watchtowers for defense"], a:0, ref:"2 Kings 17:10",verse:"And they set them up images and groves in every high hill, and under every green tree:", insight:"Every hill had an image; when everything becomes worship, nothing truly is."},
{q:"What had the LORD plainly said they must not do?", options:["Serve idols: 'Ye shall not do this thing'","Keep the sabbath in Jerusalem","Eat the passover at Bethel","Sing psalms in the high places"], a:0, ref:"2 Kings 17:12",verse:"For they served idols, whereof the LORD had said unto them, Ye shall not do this thing.", insight:"The command was plain: 'Ye shall not do this thing.' Plainness leaves no excuse."},
{q:"How did the LORD warn Israel and Judah before the fall?", options:["He testified against them by all the prophets and all the seers, saying, 'Turn ye from your evil ways'","He sent famines and plagues without any message","He raised up judges to deliver them","He shut the temple doors for a year"], a:0, ref:"2 Kings 17:13",verse:"Yet the LORD testified against Israel, and against Judah, by all the prophets, and by all the seers, saying, Turn ye from your evil ways, and keep my commandments and my statutes, according to all the law which I commanded your fathers, and which I sent to you by my servants the prophets.", insight:"Prophets and seers, again and again; God's warnings are as patient as His judgments are certain."},
{q:"How did Israel answer the LORD's warning?", options:["They would not hear, but hardened their necks","They repented and tore down the high places","They asked for more time and then reformed","They wept and returned to the covenant"], a:0, ref:"2 Kings 17:14",verse:"Notwithstanding they would not hear, but hardened their necks, like to the neck of their fathers, that did not believe in the LORD their God.", insight:"Hardened necks; the only thing stiffer than their idols was their resistance to God."},
{q:"What idols did they make and serve?", options:["Two molten calves, a grove; they worshipped the host of heaven and served Baal","One golden eagle and twelve bronze lions","Images of their ancestors and kings","Carved cherubim for the temple"], a:0, ref:"2 Kings 17:16",verse:"And they left all the commandments of the LORD their God, and made them molten images, even two calves, and made a grove, and worshipped all the host of heaven, and served Baal.", insight:"Calves, groves, stars, and Baal; they collected gods the way other nations collect tribute."},
{q:"What did they do with their sons and daughters?", options:["Caused them to pass through the fire, and used divination and enchantments","Sent them to the temple to serve the priests","Dedicated them to the king's service","Hid them from the Assyrian tax collectors"], a:0, ref:"2 Kings 17:17",verse:"And they caused their sons and their daughters to pass through the fire, and used divination and enchantments, and sold themselves to do evil in the sight of the LORD, to provoke him to anger.", insight:"They burned their own children to gods that could not hear; idolatry always ends by eating its worshippers."},
{q:"What was the LORD's final anger and judgment on Israel?", options:["Very angry, He removed them out of His sight; none were left but the tribe of Judah only","A light rebuke, and He restored them in one year","He sent them prophets for another century","He divided the kingdom into three parts"], a:0, ref:"2 Kings 17:18",verse:"Therefore the LORD was very angry with Israel, and removed them out of his sight: there was none left but the tribe of Judah only.", insight:"Removed out of His sight; the most fearful sentence in Scripture is a closed door of grace."},
{q:"What did the LORD send among the foreign settlers in Samaria?", options:["Lions, which slew some of them","A famine that lasted seven years","A plague of locusts","Serpents from the wilderness"], a:0, ref:"2 Kings 17:25",verse:"And so it was at the beginning of their dwelling there, that they feared not the LORD: therefore the LORD sent lions among them, which slew some of them.", insight:"Lions in the land they did not know how to worship in; even the ground teaches reverence when men refuse it."},
{q:"What remedy did the king of Assyria command for the lion trouble?", options:["Carry back one of the captive priests to teach them the manner of the God of the land","Send hunters to kill all the lions","Abandon the cities of Samaria entirely","Build walls around every settlement"], a:0, ref:"2 Kings 17:27",verse:"Then the king of Assyria commanded, saying, Carry thither one of the priests whom ye brought from thence; and let them go and dwell there, and let him teach them the manner of the God of the land.", insight:"A captive priest became the teacher; God uses the exiled to instruct the invaders."},
{q:"How did these nations serve in the end?", options:["They feared the LORD, and served their graven images, both they and their children","They rejected the LORD and served only Baal","They returned to Assyria and repented fully","They kept the law of Moses perfectly"], a:0, ref:"2 Kings 17:41",verse:"So these nations feared the LORD, and served their graven images, both their children, and their children’s children: as did their fathers, so do they unto this day.", insight:"Fearing the LORD while serving idols; half worship is whole rebellion."}
]},
{n:18, name:"Hezekiah Trusts the LORD", seal:{ref:"2 Kings 18:5",verse:"He trusted in the LORD God of Israel; so that after him was none like him among all the kings of Judah, nor any that were before him."}, facts:["Did you know Hezekiah smashed the bronze serpent Moses himself had made, because Israel had begun burning incense to it and even named it Nehushtan? No relic, however historic, may become an idol (2 Kings 18:4).","Did you know Hezekiah was called the most trusting king of Judah, before or after? Scripture says he trusted the LORD God of Israel 'so that after him was none like him' (2 Kings 18:5).","Did you know Rab-shakeh mocked in the Jews' own language so everyone on the wall could hear, taunting them with 'a land of corn and wine' if they would surrender (2 Kings 18:28-32)?"], questions:[
{q:"In what year of Hoshea did Hezekiah begin to reign over Judah?", options:["The third year of Hoshea son of Elah","The twelfth year of Hoshea son of Elah","The ninth year of Hoshea son of Elah","The first year of Hoshea son of Elah"], a:0, ref:"2 Kings 18:1",verse:"Now it came to pass in the third year of Hoshea son of Elah king of Israel, that Hezekiah the son of Ahaz king of Judah began to reign.", insight:"Hezekiah rose as Israel fell; God raises His best servants in the darkest hours."},
{q:"How old was Hezekiah when he began to reign, and how long did he reign?", options:["Twenty-five years old; he reigned twenty-nine years","Twenty years old; he reigned sixteen years","Thirty years old; he reigned forty years","Forty-one years old; he reigned eleven years"], a:0, ref:"2 Kings 18:2",verse:"Twenty and five years old was he when he began to reign; and he reigned twenty and nine years in Jerusalem. His mother’s name also was Abi, the daughter of Zachariah.", insight:"Twenty-nine years of doing right; a long reign is God's gift when a king spends it well."},
{q:"What was Hezekiah's mother's name?", options:["Abi, the daughter of Zachariah","Athaliah, the daughter of Ahab","Hephzibah, the daughter of Helez","Jedidah, the daughter of Adaiah"], a:0, ref:"2 Kings 18:2",verse:"Twenty and five years old was he when he began to reign; and he reigned twenty and nine years in Jerusalem. His mother’s name also was Abi, the daughter of Zachariah.", insight:"Abi the daughter of Zachariah; godly mothers shape kings the history books remember."},
{q:"What is said of Hezekiah's reign?", options:["He did that which was right in the sight of the LORD, according to all that David his father did","He did evil, but not as the kings before him","He walked in the way of the kings of Israel","He reigned well, but worshipped in the high places"], a:0, ref:"2 Kings 18:3",verse:"And he did that which was right in the sight of the LORD, according to all that David his father did.", insight:"'According to all that David his father did'; David, not Ahaz, was the pattern his son chose."},
{q:"What did Hezekiah do to the centers of false worship?", options:["He removed the high places, brake the images, and cut down the groves","He taxed the high places for the temple fund","He closed them for a year and reopened them","He left them but preached against them"], a:0, ref:"2 Kings 18:4",verse:"He removed the high places, and brake the images, and cut down the groves, and brake in pieces the brasen serpent that Moses had made: for unto those days the children of Israel did burn incense to it: and he called it Nehushtan.", insight:"Remove, break, cut down; reformation is demolition work before it is building work."},
{q:"What famous relic did Hezekiah break in pieces, and why?", options:["The brasen serpent that Moses had made, because Israel burned incense to it and called it Nehushtan","The golden calf of Bethel, because Jeroboam had made it","The altar of Ahaz, because it was pagan","The ephod of Gideon, because it became a snare"], a:0, ref:"2 Kings 18:4",verse:"He removed the high places, and brake the images, and cut down the groves, and brake in pieces the brasen serpent that Moses had made: for unto those days the children of Israel did burn incense to it: and he called it Nehushtan.", insight:"Even Moses' serpent had to go once it became an idol; no holy history excuses present idolatry."},
{q:"What is said of Hezekiah's trust in God?", options:["He trusted in the LORD God of Israel, so that after him was none like him among all the kings of Judah, nor any before him","He trusted in Egypt and in chariots","He trusted in the prophets but not in prayer","He trusted in the temple walls alone"], a:0, ref:"2 Kings 18:5",verse:"He trusted in the LORD God of Israel; so that after him was none like him among all the kings of Judah, nor any that were before him.", insight:"None like him, before or after; trust is the one contest where God keeps the records."},
{q:"What did Hezekiah do regarding the king of Assyria?", options:["He rebelled against him, and served him not","He paid him double tribute for peace","He married into his royal house","He sent his son as a hostage"], a:0, ref:"2 Kings 18:7",verse:"And the LORD was with him; and he prospered whithersoever he went forth: and he rebelled against the king of Assyria, and served him not.", insight:"He rebelled and served him not; some yokes are meant to be thrown off, not borne."},
{q:"Whom did Hezekiah smite?", options:["The Philistines, even unto Gaza, from the tower of the watchmen to the fenced city","The Edomites and the Moabites together","The Syrians of Damascus","The remnants of the Canaanites"], a:0, ref:"2 Kings 18:8",verse:"He smote the Philistines, even unto Gaza, and the borders thereof, from the tower of the watchmen to the fenced city.", insight:"He pushed the Philistines back to Gaza; a man who trusts God stops losing old ground."},
{q:"In what year did Shalmaneser come up and besiege Samaria?", options:["The fourth year of Hezekiah, the seventh year of Hoshea","The fourteenth year of Hezekiah","The first year of Hezekiah","The twentieth year of Hezekiah"], a:0, ref:"2 Kings 18:9",verse:"And it came to pass in the fourth year of king Hezekiah, which was the seventh year of Hoshea son of Elah king of Israel, that Shalmaneser king of Assyria came up against Samaria, and besieged it.", insight:"While Hezekiah reformed Judah, Samaria was falling; reform at home while judgment passes abroad."},
{q:"When was Samaria taken?", options:["At the end of three years: the sixth year of Hezekiah, the ninth year of Hoshea","In the first month of the siege","In the fourteenth year of Hezekiah","It was never taken, only besieged"], a:0, ref:"2 Kings 18:10",verse:"And at the end of three years they took it: even in the sixth year of Hezekiah, that is the ninth year of Hoshea king of Israel, Samaria was taken.", insight:"Three years of siege ended the northern kingdom; Judah watched and should have learned."},
{q:"Why was Israel carried away?", options:["They obeyed not the voice of the LORD their God, but transgressed His covenant","They trusted in the LORD too much","They refused to fight against Assyria","They kept the sabbath but not the passover"], a:0, ref:"2 Kings 18:12",verse:"Because they obeyed not the voice of the LORD their God, but transgressed his covenant, and all that Moses the servant of the LORD commanded, and would not hear them, nor do them.", insight:"Disobedience and covenant breaking; Judah is told plainly why her sister fell."},
{q:"In what year did Sennacherib come up against the fenced cities of Judah?", options:["The fourteenth year of king Hezekiah","The fourth year of king Hezekiah","The twenty-ninth year of king Hezekiah","The first year of king Hezekiah"], a:0, ref:"2 Kings 18:13",verse:"Now in the fourteenth year of king Hezekiah did Sennacherib king of Assyria come up against all the fenced cities of Judah, and took them.", insight:"The fourteenth year; testing comes to the faithful too, even after reformation."},
{q:"What message did Hezekiah send to the king of Assyria at Lachish?", options:["I have offended; return from me: that which thou puttest on me will I bear","I have done no wrong; fight me if thou darest","I surrender my crown and my kingdom","I will join thee against Egypt"], a:0, ref:"2 Kings 18:14",verse:"And Hezekiah king of Judah sent to the king of Assyria to Lachish, saying, I have offended; return from me: that which thou puttest on me will I bear. And the king of Assyria appointed unto Hezekiah king of Judah three hundred talents of silver and thirty talents of gold.", insight:"'I have offended'; Hezekiah's confession was honest, though his payment was a mistake."},
{q:"What did Hezekiah give to the king of Assyria?", options:["All the silver found in the house of the LORD and in the king's treasures","Only the tribute of the coastal cities","The golden vessels of the temple court","His own crown and royal robes"], a:0, ref:"2 Kings 18:15",verse:"And Hezekiah gave him all the silver that was found in the house of the LORD, and in the treasures of the king’s house.", insight:"He emptied God's house to pay a bully; fear pays ransoms that faith would never owe."},
{q:"What did Hezekiah cut off to satisfy Assyria?", options:["The gold from the doors of the temple of the LORD, and from the pillars he had overlaid","The silver trumpets of the priests","The bronze laver and its stand","The curtains of the tabernacle"], a:0, ref:"2 Kings 18:16",verse:"At that time did Hezekiah cut off the gold from the doors of the temple of the LORD, and from the pillars which Hezekiah king of Judah had overlaid, and gave it to the king of Assyria.", insight:"He stripped the doors he had gilded himself; fear undoes in a day what devotion built over years."},
{q:"Which three officers did the king of Assyria send against Jerusalem?", options:["Tartan, Rabsaris, and Rab-shakeh","Tiglath, Pul, and Shalmaneser","Eliakim, Shebna, and Joah","The Tartan, the Rabsaris, and the cupbearer"], a:0, ref:"2 Kings 18:17",verse:"And the king of Assyria sent Tartan and Rabsaris and Rab-shakeh from Lachish to king Hezekiah with a great host against Jerusalem. And they went up and came to Jerusalem. And when they were come up, they came and stood by the conduit of the upper pool, which is in the highway of the fuller’s field.", insight:"Great titles marched against the city; intimidation is Assyria's first weapon."},
{q:"Who came out to meet the Assyrian officers?", options:["Eliakim the son of Hilkiah, Shebna the scribe, and Joah the son of Asaph the recorder","Hezekiah himself with his captains","Isaiah the prophet with the elders","The priests with the ark of God"], a:0, ref:"2 Kings 18:18",verse:"And when they had called to the king, there came out to them Eliakim the son of Hilkiah, which was over the household, and Shebna the scribe, and Joah the son of Asaph the recorder.", insight:"The steward, the scribe, the recorder; wise kings send wise men to face proud enemies."},
{q:"What did Rab-shakeh call Egypt?", options:["The staff of this bruised reed, which pierces the hand of him that leans on it","A strong tower that never falls","A faithful friend in time of war","A golden shield for the oppressed"], a:0, ref:"2 Kings 18:21",verse:"Now, behold, thou trustest upon the staff of this bruised reed, even upon Egypt, on which if a man lean, it will go into his hand, and pierce it: so is Pharaoh king of Egypt unto all that trust on him.", insight:"A bruised reed; even the enemy knew Egypt's help was a wound disguised as a crutch."},
{q:"How did the people answer Rab-shakeh's taunts?", options:["They held their peace, and answered him not a word, for the king commanded it","They shouted curses back at him","They wept aloud on the wall","They opened the gates and surrendered"], a:0, ref:"2 Kings 18:36",verse:"But the people held their peace, and answered him not a word: for the king’s commandment was, saying, Answer him not.", insight:"They held their peace; silence under orders is stronger than shouting without them."}
]},
{n:19, name:"The Angel and the Assyrians", seal:{ref:"2 Kings 19:35",verse:"And it came to pass that night, that the angel of the LORD went out, and smote in the camp of the Assyrians an hundred fourscore and five thousand: and when they arose early in the morning, behold, they were all dead corpses."}, facts:["Did you know Hezekiah's prayer was physical? He took Sennacherib's threatening letter, climbed to the temple, and spread it out before the LORD like evidence in a courtroom (2 Kings 19:14).","Did you know God's answer treated the boaster as a tool? 'I know thy abode, and thy going out, and thy coming in, and thy rage against me... I will put my hook in thy nose' (2 Kings 19:27-28).","Did you know Sennacherib died exactly as Isaiah prophesied, struck down by his own sons while worshipping his god Nisroch in Nineveh (2 Kings 19:7,37)?"], questions:[
{q:"What did Hezekiah do when he heard Rab-shakeh's words?", options:["He rent his clothes, covered himself with sackcloth, and went into the house of the LORD","He gathered his army for battle at once","He shut the gates and sent for Egypt","He fled from Jerusalem to the wilderness"], a:0, ref:"2 Kings 19:1",verse:"And it came to pass, when king Hezekiah heard it, that he rent his clothes, and covered himself with sackcloth, and went into the house of the LORD.", insight:"Rent clothes and sackcloth; the first move of faith in a crisis is toward God, not toward the war room."},
{q:"Whom did Hezekiah send to Isaiah the prophet?", options:["Eliakim, Shebna the scribe, and the elders of the priests, covered with sackcloth","His captains and all his mighty men","The priests with sacrifices and offerings","His own sons to intercede for him"], a:0, ref:"2 Kings 19:2",verse:"And he sent Eliakim, which was over the household, and Shebna the scribe, and the elders of the priests, covered with sackcloth, to Isaiah the prophet the son of Amoz.", insight:"He sent his highest officials in sackcloth; humble leaders send humble messengers."},
{q:"How did Hezekiah describe that day?", options:["A day of trouble, and of rebuke, and blasphemy: the children are come to the birth, and there is not strength to bring forth","A day of feasting and of gladness","A day like any other in Jerusalem","A day of harvest and of peace"], a:0, ref:"2 Kings 19:3",verse:"And they said unto him, Thus saith Hezekiah, This day is a day of trouble, and of rebuke, and blasphemy: for the children are come to the birth, and there is not strength to bring forth.", insight:"Children at the birth with no strength to bring forth; trouble feels like labor with no delivery, until God acts."},
{q:"What did Isaiah say to Hezekiah's servants?", options:["Be not afraid of the words thou hast heard, with which the servants of the king of Assyria have blasphemed me","Flee the city before the morning","Make peace with Assyria at any price","Gather the people to the temple to fast"], a:0, ref:"2 Kings 19:6",verse:"And Isaiah said unto them, Thus shall ye say to your master, Thus saith the LORD, Be not afraid of the words which thou hast heard, with which the servants of the king of Assyria have blasphemed me.", insight:"'Be not afraid of the words'; blasphemy sounds loud, but heaven has already answered it."},
{q:"What would God send upon Sennacherib?", options:["A blast; he shall hear a rumour, return to his own land, and fall by the sword in his own land","A plague upon all his cities at once","A famine that would waste his army","An earthquake under his palace"], a:0, ref:"2 Kings 19:7",verse:"Behold, I will send a blast upon him, and he shall hear a rumour, and shall return to his own land; and I will cause him to fall by the sword in his own land.", insight:"A rumour and a blast; God needs no army to turn an emperor around."},
{q:"Where did Rab-shakeh find the king of Assyria?", options:["Warring against Libnah, having departed from Lachish","Besieging Jerusalem with all his host","Returned already to Nineveh","Camped at the brook Kidron"], a:0, ref:"2 Kings 19:8",verse:"So Rab-shakeh returned, and found the king of Assyria warring against Libnah: for he had heard that he was departed from Lachish.", insight:"He had moved from Lachish to Libnah; proud men keep busy while God prepares their end."},
{q:"Who was Tirhakah?", options:["The king of Ethiopia, come out to fight against the Assyrian","The king of Egypt, an ally of Hezekiah","A captain of Sennacherib's own guard","The king of Babylon, seeking tribute"], a:0, ref:"2 Kings 19:9",verse:"And when he heard say of Tirhakah king of Ethiopia, Behold, he is come out to fight against thee: he sent messengers again unto Hezekiah, saying,", insight:"Ethiopia marched against Assyria; God stirs distant nations to trouble the proud."},
{q:"What did Sennacherib's letter warn Hezekiah?", options:["Let not thy God, in whom thou trustest, deceive thee, saying, Jerusalem shall not be delivered","Thy God has forsaken thee and joined with me","Surrender now and I will spare the temple","Thy prophets have lied to thee from the first"], a:0, ref:"2 Kings 19:10",verse:"Thus shall ye speak to Hezekiah king of Judah, saying, Let not thy God in whom thou trustest deceive thee, saying, Jerusalem shall not be delivered into the hand of the king of Assyria.", insight:"'Let not thy God deceive thee'; the enemy's oldest trick is to make you doubt God's faithfulness."},
{q:"What did Hezekiah do with Sennacherib's letter?", options:["Went up into the house of the LORD, and spread it before the LORD","Burned it in the fire before his servants","Hid it in the king's treasury","Sent it back with a defiant answer"], a:0, ref:"2 Kings 19:14",verse:"And Hezekiah received the letter of the hand of the messengers, and read it: and Hezekiah went up into the house of the LORD, and spread it before the LORD.", insight:"He spread the letter before the LORD; lay the threat itself on heaven's table and let God read it."},
{q:"How did Hezekiah address God in his prayer?", options:["O LORD God of Israel, which dwellest between the cherubims, thou art the God, even thou alone, of all the kingdoms of the earth","O God of Abraham, remember thy servant","O LORD, thou God of the temple only","O mighty God, thou God of Judah alone"], a:0, ref:"2 Kings 19:15",verse:"And Hezekiah prayed before the LORD, and said, O LORD God of Israel, which dwellest between the cherubims, thou art the God, even thou alone, of all the kingdoms of the earth: thou hast made heaven and earth.", insight:"'Thou alone, of all the kingdoms of the earth'; true prayer starts by remembering how big God is."},
{q:"What did Hezekiah ask God to do?", options:["Bow down thine ear and hear; open thine eyes and see; hear the words of Sennacherib","Send fire from heaven this night","Shut the mouths of the prophets","Give him wisdom above Solomon"], a:0, ref:"2 Kings 19:16",verse:"LORD, bow down thine ear, and hear: open, LORD, thine eyes, and see: and hear the words of Sennacherib, which hath sent him to reproach the living God.", insight:"Bow down, open, hear; Hezekiah asked God to look, and God always answers that prayer."},
{q:"Why had the Assyrians been able to destroy the gods of the nations?", options:["They were no gods, but the work of men's hands, wood and stone","They were weaker gods than the LORD","Their priests had forsaken them","They had been abandoned by their kings"], a:0, ref:"2 Kings 19:18",verse:"And have cast their gods into the fire: for they were no gods, but the work of men’s hands, wood and stone: therefore they have destroyed them.", insight:"Wood and stone; Hezekiah saw what the nations could not: their gods were furniture."},
{q:"For what purpose did Hezekiah ask to be saved?", options:["That all the kingdoms of the earth may know that thou art the LORD God, even thou only","That his own name would be great","That Judah might rule over Assyria","That the temple treasures would be safe"], a:0, ref:"2 Kings 19:19",verse:"Now therefore, O LORD our God, I beseech thee, save thou us out of his hand, that all the kingdoms of the earth may know that thou art the LORD God, even thou only.", insight:"He asked deliverance for God's glory, not his own; prayers aimed at God's fame are always heard."},
{q:"What did the LORD say He had heard?", options:["That which thou hast prayed to me against Sennacherib king of Assyria","The weeping of the people on the wall","The taunts of Rab-shakeh alone","The counsel of the princes of Judah"], a:0, ref:"2 Kings 19:20",verse:"Then Isaiah the son of Amoz sent to Hezekiah, saying, Thus saith the LORD God of Israel, That which thou hast prayed to me against Sennacherib king of Assyria I have heard.", insight:"'I have heard'; every prayer laid before God is answered prayer, whether we see it yet or not."},
{q:"How did the virgin daughter of Zion treat Sennacherib?", options:["She despised him and laughed him to scorn; the daughter of Jerusalem shook her head at him","She wept and fled before him","She opened her gates to him","She paid him tribute in gold"], a:0, ref:"2 Kings 19:21",verse:"This is the word that the LORD hath spoken concerning him; The virgin the daughter of Zion hath despised thee, and laughed thee to scorn; the daughter of Jerusalem hath shaken her head at thee.", insight:"Despised and laughed to scorn; when God fights for a city, the city can afford to laugh."},
{q:"Against whom had Sennacherib exalted his voice?", options:["Against the Holy One of Israel","Against the king of Judah only","Against the gods of the nations","Against his own servants"], a:0, ref:"2 Kings 19:22",verse:"Whom hast thou reproached and blasphemed? and against whom hast thou exalted thy voice, and lifted up thine eyes on high? even against the Holy One of Israel.", insight:"His rage was against the Holy One; mock a king and you face armies, mock God and you face heaven."},
{q:"What sign did God give Hezekiah?", options:["This year eat what grows of itself, the second year the same, and the third year sow, reap, and plant vineyards","The sun would stand still for a day","The dead in the valley would rise","A star would appear over Jerusalem"], a:0, ref:"2 Kings 19:29",verse:"And this shall be a sign unto thee, Ye shall eat this year such things as grow of themselves, and in the second year that which springeth of the same; and in the third year sow ye, and reap, and plant vineyards, and eat the fruits thereof.", insight:"Eat, then sow and reap; God feeds His people through the famine and restores their fields after it."},
{q:"What would the escaped remnant of Judah do?", options:["Take root downward, and bear fruit upward","Flee to Egypt and hide","Build a new temple in Samaria","Return to the high places"], a:0, ref:"2 Kings 19:30",verse:"And the remnant that is escaped of the house of Judah shall yet again take root downward, and bear fruit upward.", insight:"Root downward, fruit upward; the order never changes: depth first, then harvest."},
{q:"What would the king of Assyria NOT do to Jerusalem?", options:["Come into the city, nor shoot an arrow there, nor come before it with shield, nor cast a bank against it","Send messengers with letters","Threaten the people on the wall","Boast against the God of Israel"], a:0, ref:"2 Kings 19:32",verse:"Therefore thus saith the LORD concerning the king of Assyria, He shall not come into this city, nor shoot an arrow there, nor come before it with shield, nor cast a bank against it.", insight:"Not one arrow; when God says no, the mightiest army cannot draw a bow."},
{q:"How many did the angel of the LORD smite in the Assyrian camp that night?", options:["An hundred fourscore and five thousand","Ten thousand","Fifty thousand","Three thousand"], a:0, ref:"2 Kings 19:35",verse:"And it came to pass that night, that the angel of the LORD went out, and smote in the camp of the Assyrians an hundred fourscore and five thousand: and when they arose early in the morning, behold, they were all dead corpses.", insight:"One angel, one night, 185,000; heaven's arithmetic humbles every earthly army."}
]},
{n:20, name:"Hezekiah's Prayer Answered", seal:{ref:"2 Kings 20:5",verse:"Turn again, and tell Hezekiah the captain of my people, Thus saith the LORD, the God of David thy father, I have heard thy prayer, I have seen thy tears: behold, I will heal thee: on the third day thou shalt go up unto the house of the LORD."}, facts:["Did you know Hezekiah's prayer turned a death sentence around before Isaiah reached the courtyard? God's answer came 'afore Isaiah was gone out into the middle court' (2 Kings 20:4-5).","Did you know Hezekiah himself chose the sign, calling a forward-moving shadow 'a light thing' and asking instead for the shadow to go back ten degrees (2 Kings 20:10-11)?","Did you know Hezekiah's pool and conduit brought water into Jerusalem through a rock tunnel, the famous tunnel of Hezekiah that still carries water today (2 Kings 20:20)."], questions:[
{q:"What happened to Hezekiah 'in those days'?", options:["He was sick unto death","He went up against Babylon","He rebuilt the temple walls","He fled to Egypt"], a:0, ref:"2 Kings 20:1",verse:"In those days was Hezekiah sick unto death. And the prophet Isaiah the son of Amoz came to him, and said unto him, Thus saith the LORD, Set thine house in order; for thou shalt die, and not live.", insight:"Sick unto death; even the king who trusted God most was not spared the sickbed."},
{q:"What word did Isaiah bring to Hezekiah?", options:["Set thine house in order; for thou shalt die, and not live","The LORD will heal thee on the third day","Fear not, for thou shalt live forty years","Go down to the brook and wash seven times"], a:0, ref:"2 Kings 20:1",verse:"In those days was Hezekiah sick unto death. And the prophet Isaiah the son of Amoz came to him, and said unto him, Thus saith the LORD, Set thine house in order; for thou shalt die, and not live.", insight:"'Set thine house in order'; the hardest word of God is also the kindest, because it tells the truth."},
{q:"What did Hezekiah do when he heard the word?", options:["He turned his face to the wall, and prayed unto the LORD","He called his captains to make war","He tore his royal robes in despair","He sent for the physicians of Egypt"], a:0, ref:"2 Kings 20:2",verse:"Then he turned his face to the wall, and prayed unto the LORD, saying,", insight:"He turned his face to the wall; sometimes prayer needs a wall between you and the world."},
{q:"What did Hezekiah ask the LORD to remember?", options:["How I have walked before thee in truth and with a perfect heart, and have done that which is good in thy sight","The riches he had given to the temple","The battles he had won for Judah","The tribute he had paid to Assyria"], a:0, ref:"2 Kings 20:3",verse:"I beseech thee, O LORD, remember now how I have walked before thee in truth and with a perfect heart, and have done that which is good in thy sight. And Hezekiah wept sore.", insight:"He pled his walk, not his worth; a clear conscience is the boldest prayer book."},
{q:"How did Hezekiah weep?", options:["He wept sore","He wept quietly in secret","He shed no tears at all","He wept only before his servants"], a:0, ref:"2 Kings 20:3",verse:"I beseech thee, O LORD, remember now how I have walked before thee in truth and with a perfect heart, and have done that which is good in thy sight. And Hezekiah wept sore.", insight:"He wept sore; God is not embarrassed by loud tears from an honest heart."},
{q:"How soon did God's answer come?", options:["Afore Isaiah was gone out into the middle court","After seven days of fasting","In the third year afterward","Only after Hezekiah recovered"], a:0, ref:"2 Kings 20:4",verse:"And it came to pass, afore Isaiah was gone out into the middle court, that the word of the LORD came to him, saying,", insight:"Before the prophet reached the courtyard; heaven answers faster than messengers can walk."},
{q:"What did the LORD say to Hezekiah?", options:["I have heard thy prayer, I have seen thy tears: behold, I will heal thee","Thy time is come; prepare to die","I have forgotten thy works","Ask another king to pray for thee"], a:0, ref:"2 Kings 20:5",verse:"Turn again, and tell Hezekiah the captain of my people, Thus saith the LORD, the God of David thy father, I have heard thy prayer, I have seen thy tears: behold, I will heal thee: on the third day thou shalt go up unto the house of the LORD.", insight:"Heard prayer, seen tears; God keeps both an ear and an eye on His suffering saints."},
{q:"When would Hezekiah go up to the house of the LORD?", options:["On the third day","On the seventh day","In the third month","After forty days"], a:0, ref:"2 Kings 20:8",verse:"And Hezekiah said unto Isaiah, What shall be the sign that the LORD will heal me, and that I shall go up into the house of the LORD the third day?", insight:"On the third day; God loves third days, and they usually mean resurrection."},
{q:"How many years did God add to Hezekiah's life?", options:["Fifteen years","Seven years","Ten years","Twenty years"], a:0, ref:"2 Kings 20:6",verse:"And I will add unto thy days fifteen years; and I will deliver thee and this city out of the hand of the king of Assyria; and I will defend this city for mine own sake, and for my servant David’s sake.", insight:"Fifteen years; prayer does not just comfort the dying, it can rewrite their calendar."},
{q:"What else did God promise Hezekiah?", options:["Deliverance of him and the city from the king of Assyria; God would defend the city","The throne of David forever in his line","Victory over Babylon and Egypt","Riches greater than Solomon's"], a:0, ref:"2 Kings 20:6",verse:"And I will add unto thy days fifteen years; and I will deliver thee and this city out of the hand of the king of Assyria; and I will defend this city for mine own sake, and for my servant David’s sake.", insight:"Deliverance thrown in with healing; God answers the prayer you prayed and the fear you did not name."},
{q:"What remedy did Isaiah prescribe?", options:["Take a lump of figs, and lay it on the boil, and he recovered","Drink water from the pool of Siloam","Anoint him with holy oil seven times","Wash in Jordan seven times"], a:0, ref:"2 Kings 20:7",verse:"And Isaiah said, Take a lump of figs. And they took and laid it on the boil, and he recovered.", insight:"A lump of figs; God often heals through the ordinary things He already made."},
{q:"What sign did Hezekiah ask Isaiah for?", options:["A sign that the LORD will heal me, and that I shall go up into the house of the LORD the third day","A sign that Assyria would fall that night","A sign written in the stars","A sign from the Urim and Thummim"], a:0, ref:"2 Kings 20:8",verse:"And Hezekiah said unto Isaiah, What shall be the sign that the LORD will heal me, and that I shall go up into the house of the LORD the third day?", insight:"He asked for a sign of healing; asking God to confirm His promise is faith, not doubt."},
{q:"What choice did Isaiah offer Hezekiah?", options:["Shall the shadow go forward ten degrees, or go back ten degrees?","Shall the sun stand still or the moon delay?","Shall it rain or shall the wind cease?","Shall the stars fall or the sea part?"], a:0, ref:"2 Kings 20:9",verse:"And Isaiah said, This sign shalt thou have of the LORD, that the LORD will do the thing that he hath spoken: shall the shadow go forward ten degrees, or go back ten degrees?", insight:"Forward or backward; God let the sick man choose the miracle's direction."},
{q:"What did Hezekiah choose?", options:["Let the shadow return backward ten degrees, for going forward is a light thing","Let the shadow go forward ten degrees","Let there be no sign at all","Let the night become as day"], a:0, ref:"2 Kings 20:10",verse:"And Hezekiah answered, It is a light thing for the shadow to go down ten degrees: nay, but let the shadow return backward ten degrees.", insight:"He chose the harder sign; great faith asks for the unmistakable, not the easy."},
{q:"What happened to the shadow?", options:["It was brought ten degrees backward, by which it had gone down in the dial of Ahaz","It stood still for a whole day","It vanished until the evening","It moved forward twenty degrees"], a:0, ref:"2 Kings 20:11",verse:"And Isaiah the prophet cried unto the LORD: and he brought the shadow ten degrees backward, by which it had gone down in the dial of Ahaz.", insight:"On Ahaz's own dial; the sign of mercy was written on the instrument of Hezekiah's wicked father."},
{q:"Who sent letters and a present to Hezekiah?", options:["Berodach-baladan, the son of Baladan, king of Babylon","Tirhakah, the king of Ethiopia","So, the king of Egypt","The king of Tyre, seeking trade"], a:0, ref:"2 Kings 20:12",verse:"At that time Berodach-baladan, the son of Baladan, king of Babylon, sent letters and a present unto Hezekiah: for he had heard that Hezekiah had been sick.", insight:"Babylon sent gifts; kindness from future enemies is still worth watching."},
{q:"Why did the king of Babylon send to Hezekiah?", options:["He had heard that Hezekiah had been sick","He wanted an alliance against Assyria","He sought tribute from Judah","He had heard of the sundial miracle"], a:0, ref:"2 Kings 20:12",verse:"At that time Berodach-baladan, the son of Baladan, king of Babylon, sent letters and a present unto Hezekiah: for he had heard that Hezekiah had been sick.", insight:"He heard of the sickness; news of your weakness travels farther than you think."},
{q:"What did Hezekiah show the Babylonian messengers?", options:["All the house of his precious things: the silver, the gold, the spices, the precious ointment, the armour, all his treasures","Only the temple courts from a distance","Nothing, for he refused to see them","The city walls and the gates"], a:0, ref:"2 Kings 20:13",verse:"And Hezekiah hearkened unto them, and shewed them all the house of his precious things, the silver, and the gold, and the spices, and the precious ointment, and all the house of his armour, and all that was found in his treasures: there was nothing in his house, nor in all his dominion, that Hezekiah shewed them not.", insight:"He showed them everything; pride loves an audience, and audiences remember what they see."},
{q:"What did Isaiah prophesy about Hezekiah's treasures?", options:["All that is in thine house shall be carried into Babylon: nothing shall be left","They would be hidden and never found","They would be doubled in his lifetime","Assyria would take them within the year"], a:0, ref:"2 Kings 20:17",verse:"Behold, the days come, that all that is in thine house, and that which thy fathers have laid up in store unto this day, shall be carried into Babylon: nothing shall be left, saith the LORD.", insight:"'Nothing shall be left'; showing off your blessings can forfeit them."},
{q:"How did Hezekiah receive Isaiah's hard word?", options:["Good is the word of the LORD which thou hast spoken; is it not good, if peace and truth be in my days?","He repented and tore his clothes","He was angry and imprisoned Isaiah","He wept and pleaded for his sons"], a:0, ref:"2 Kings 20:19",verse:"Then said Hezekiah unto Isaiah, Good is the word of the LORD which thou hast spoken. And he said, Is it not good, if peace and truth be in my days?", insight:"'Peace in my days'; contentment with judgment postponed is the saddest sentence Hezekiah ever spoke."}
]}
]);
